# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Middle Strategy — English reading."""

TITLE = 'Book Two — The Middle Strategy'
HEAD = '''Twenty-one passages, the shortest of the three, and the only one in which the book turns round and describes itself. The first half quotes the <i>Military Dispositions</i> five times — another lost book, of which again only what is quoted here survives. Then the text breaks off and says: “<i>the Three Strategies was written for an age in decline</i>,” and sets out, book by book, what the Upper, Middle and Lower Strategies are for. That is an unusual thing for an early Chinese book to do: write its own abstract, in its own middle chapter. What follows is more unusual still. “When the high birds are gone the good bow is put away; when the enemy state is destroyed the planning minister is finished.” This is not about winning battles. It is about surviving one’s own side afterwards — “a minister who understands the <i>Middle Strategy</i> thoroughly can keep both his achievement and his life.” That sentence is where the <i>Three Strategies</i> sits in the literature of Chinese warfare: it writes the meritorious servant a way out. The scroll ends with four words — <i>and so rulers keep it secret</i>.'''

PARA = {
 'j2-1': 'The Three Sovereigns said nothing, and their influence flowed through the four seas; so the world did not know whom to thank. (Four descending grades follow — sovereign, emperor, king, hegemon — each one less than the last.)',
 'j2-2': 'The emperor takes his body from heaven and his rule from earth; he speaks and gives orders, and the world is at peace. His ministers yield the credit to one another, the four seas are transformed, and the people do not know why it is so. So he need not wait on ceremony and reward to use a minister; merit is a good thing and does no harm. (For “his ministers” both witnesses read “ruler and ministers.” The phrase “a good thing and does no harm” returns once more below.)',
 'j2-3': 'The king governs men by the Way; he brings hearts down and makes wills submit, lays out standards against decay, brings the four seas to assembly, and does not let the king’s office lapse. He has arms and armour ready, and yet no trouble of battle. The lord does not suspect the minister, the minister does not suspect the lord; the state is settled and the lord is safe, and a minister who withdraws on a point of duty can likewise do good and no harm. (Both witnesses omit “has” before “arms and armour.”)',
 'j2-4': 'The hegemon governs his officers by leverage, binds them by good faith, and employs them by reward. Let the good faith weaken and the officers draw away; let the reward fall short and they will not obey. (The descent ends here. What follows — the five passages from the <i>Military Dispositions</i> — is the hegemon’s way of handling men.)',
 'j2-5': 'The <i>Military Dispositions</i> says: “When the army marches out, the general must have it in his own hands. If advance and retreat are worked from within the palace, the thing will hardly succeed.”',
 'j2-6': 'The <i>Military Dispositions</i> says: “Use the clever, use the brave, use the greedy, use the stupid. The clever delight in establishing merit, the brave in having their way, the greedy race after gain, the stupid have no regard for death. Use each according to the passion he actually has: this is the finest leverage in war.” (It does not propose to improve anybody. It proposes to put each man where his nature already points.)',
 'j2-7': 'The <i>Military Dispositions</i> says: “Do not let a fluent man discuss the enemy’s merits, for fear he unsettle the multitude. Do not put a kind man in charge of money, for fear he give too much away and men attach themselves to him.”',
 'j2-8': 'The <i>Military Dispositions</i> says: “Forbid the shamans and diviners; they are not to divine the army’s fortune for officers and men.” (One sentence, and a weighty one — an explicit refusal of divination is rare in a military book.)',
 'j2-9': 'The <i>Military Dispositions</i> says: “A man of principle is not to be employed with money. For the man of principle will not die for one who is not humane, and the clever man will not plan for a benighted lord.” (Set this against “the greedy race after gain” above: the greedy are handled with money, and the man of principle is exactly the one who cannot be.)',
 'j2-10': 'A ruler may not be without virtue: without virtue his ministers rebel. He may not be without authority: without authority he loses his hold.',
 'j2-11': 'A minister may not be without virtue: without virtue he has no means of serving his lord. He may not be without authority: without authority the state is weak — yet with too much of it he brings himself down. (The seed of the second half of the scroll, where the subject becomes how to survive.)',
 'j2-12': 'So the sage kings governed the age by watching rise and decline and weighing gain and loss, and made their institutions accordingly: two divisions for a feudal lord, three for a regional chief, six for the Son of Heaven. When the age falls into disorder, rebellion is born; when the king’s favour is exhausted, those bound by oath fall to killing one another.',
 'j2-13': 'Where virtue is matched and power is even and neither can overturn the other, then one takes hold of the hearts of the heroes and shares the likes and dislikes of the many, and only then adds the arts of contrivance. For without stratagem there is no settling a doubt; without the unexpected there is no breaking a conspiracy or ending a raid; without secret scheming there is no success. (“Without secret scheming there is no success” is said without apology, and is one of the things the book has been held against.)',
 'j2-14': 'The sage takes his model from heaven, the worthy from earth, the clever from antiquity. (The hinge. From here the book speaks about itself.)',
 'j2-15': 'And so the <i>Three Strategies</i> was written for an age in decline. The <i>Upper Strategy</i> sets out ceremony and reward, distinguishes the treacherous from the heroic, and marks what succeeds and what fails. The <i>Middle Strategy</i> grades conduct and examines the arts of contrivance. The <i>Lower Strategy</i> expounds the Way and virtue, examines safety and danger, and makes plain the guilt of injuring the worthy. (A book writing its own abstract in its own middle chapter is a rare thing. So is a book saying openly that it was written for a bad age.)',
 'j2-16': 'So a ruler who understands the <i>Upper Strategy</i> thoroughly can employ the worthy and take his enemies; one who understands the <i>Middle Strategy</i> can manage his generals and command the multitude; one who understands the <i>Lower Strategy</i> can see where rise and decline come from and make out the ordering principle of a state.',
 'j2-17': 'A minister who understands the <i>Middle Strategy</i> thoroughly can keep both his achievement and his life. (A single sentence standing alone. The ruler uses the same book to manage his generals; the minister uses it to stay alive.)',
 'j2-18': 'When the high birds are gone, the good bow is put away; when the enemy state is destroyed, the planning minister is finished. “Finished” does not mean he loses his life; it means his authority is taken and his power abolished. (The proverb is already in Fan Li’s letter to Wen Zhong in the <i>Records of the Historian</i>. This is the most famous passage in the book, and the reason it is so often said to have been written for Zhang Liang.)',
 'j2-19': 'He is enfeoffed at court and raised to the height a subject can reach, so as to display his merit; given a good fief in the central provinces, so as to enrich his house; given beauties and rare things, so as to please his heart. (Three things given — and the passage before has just said what is taken.)',
 'j2-20': 'A multitude once brought together cannot be scattered in a day; authority once handed over cannot be taken back in a day. The hour when the army is dismissed and the troops disbanded is the hinge of survival and ruin. So he is weakened by a title and stripped by a fief: this is called the hegemon’s strategy. Hence the hegemon’s conduct, argued over, always comes out mixed. (Both witnesses invert “authority” to “authoritative power.” The book’s own verdict on the hegemon is that no clean account of him can be given.)',
 'j2-21': 'The power to keep the altars of state and net the heroes is what the <i>Middle Strategy</i> is; and so rulers of the age keep it secret. (Both witnesses read “rulers of power” rather than “rulers of the age,” which follows on from the clause before. The base reading is kept.)',
}
